{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/reference/gs/state.md at exact 7f45a0aa240b3d1c4f9499e01f0a6817ac21de3b on request/submit-deadline-override, under promise git:sha1…a7ab3ab on request git:sha1…d3742a6\n\nRepublished after the changes-requested verdict b5e3c40c. Every item the review\nraised is closed here, and the two security defects were reproduced before being\nfixed rather than taken on the reviewer's word.\n\nS1, the batch recovery advice could cause a duplicate durable append. The\nrefusal said \"run the same file again — key X replays what landed\" whenever the\nact that timed out carried a key. Replay safety is not a property of that act: an\nact given no key lands afresh, as this package's own account of batch recovery\nsays, so a file whose first act carried no key would gain a second copy of it by\nfollowing the advice. The refusal now weighs every act at or before the failure\nand says which of two cases the author is in: the file is safe to rerun, or these\npositions carry no key and would land again. docs/reference/gs/batch.md carried\nthe same unconditional claim and is corrected in the same head.\n\nS2, the derived transport backstop could become no backstop. GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE\nof 2562047h47m15s is positive and accepted, and adding the five-second margin\nwrapped it to -2562047h47m13.709551616s, which net/http reads as no timeout at\nall — strictly worse than the fixed forty seconds it replaced. I reproduced the\narithmetic before changing anything. The addition now saturates, and\nTestBackstopOutlastsEvenAnAbsurdDeadline holds the bound at four call deadlines\nincluding the maximum.\n\nC1, the context binding was proven for one command in twelve. The reviewer made\ngs promise resolve the deadline and throw it away, and the package stayed green.\nTestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline now asks all twelve in turn, because\nthe binding is one line per command and a missing line is silent.\n\nC2, TimedOut's Timeout() call had no red proof: the test's refusal list contained\nno net.Error at all. It now contains a refused dial as a real *net.OpError\nwrapping ECONNREFUSED, which is the case that matters — a refused dial must never\nbe read as an expiry, because nothing was appended and the author would be sent\nlooking for something that is not there.\n\nC3, the adapter repair was incomplete: the body was testable but the call in\nmain() was not, and the original mutation survived one line further up. The\nresolution moved into newServer, which every server in this package goes through,\nand the refusal it may carry is answered by run() before the adapter serves\nanything. configureResidentDeadlines is gone rather than patched.\n\nC4, the GitHub connector had no test and let a malformed value start the run and\nfail at the append. It now resolves before any observation is made, carries the\nvalue on the run's context, and two tests hold it: one that an unreadable value\nrefuses the run, and one that a stalled resident is left at this run's deadline\nrather than the default.\n\nC5, gs publish advertises --deadline and its resident read kept a ten-second\nliteral. It now reads under the same deadline, with a red proof.\n\nC6, submitDeadline guarded against a non-positive value that its only writer\nrefuses. The guard and the half-test pinning it are gone.\n\nTwo further gaps surfaced while proving the repairs, and both were mine: the\nbatch end-to-end test used a single act, so a refusal built from the failing act\nalone was indistinguishable from one built from the file — it now lands the first\nact against a real in-process resident and stalls the second; and the connector's\ndial had the same hole. Both have mutations in the table below.\n\n\nConditions met at this exact head.\n\n1. Every command that files a durable act accepts an explicit submit deadline.\nThe twelve that append — state, promise, artifact, review, review-request,\nratify, supersede, reassign-if-unclaimed, batch, merge, land and publish — take\n--deadline; the docset surface gate reads the flags out of the source, so that\nlist is the implementation's own answer rather than mine. GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE\nsupplies the value when the flag is absent, the flag wins where both are given,\nand with neither the deadline is the same ten seconds every caller had before.\n\n2. One place decides it. residentclient.ResolveSubmitDeadline is that place, and\nsubmitRequest, the GitHub connector's submit and the MCP adapter's call deadline\nall take their answer from it; no call site carries its own literal any more. A\nvalue that is not a positive duration is refused, naming what it would accept\n(\"write a positive duration, such as 30s or 2m\"), rather than being replaced by\nthe default the author was trying to escape.\n\nThe adapter needed two further changes to make that honest. Its transport\nbackstop was a fixed forty seconds, which would have cut any call deadline\nraised past it; it is now derived from the longest deadline in the policy, so\nraising the deadline is honoured rather than silently clipped. And the startup\nwiring moved out of main() into configureResidentDeadlines, because a line no\ntest can call is a line nobody has checked.\n\n3. gs batch passes the same deadline to each of its per-act submissions, and its\nrefusal on a timed-out act says what recovery needs: the act may already have\nlanded, and the key to replay under is named. An act with no idempotency_key\ncannot be replayed at all — gs state mints a random key, so a second run appends\na second copy — so that refusal says to find the act first instead. A refused act\nkeeps its own words: telling an author their act may have landed when the\nresident said it did not would be worse than saying nothing.\n\n4. Tests pin each of those. The default is ten seconds; the flag and the variable\neach raise it and the flag wins; unparsable, zero and negative values are all\nrefused; a stalled resident is left to show that the deadline reaches the dial\nrather than merely the context, at two different values; a batch act whose\ndeadline expires reports the key to replay under; and the pinned MCP deadline\ntest now reads the shared source instead of a second literal of its own.\n\n5. Eighteen mutations of the new guards, each turning a named test red, the\nwhole table in 109 seconds:\n\n  explicit value beats the environment: TestResolveSubmitDeadlinePrefersExplicitThenEnvironment\n  a value that cannot be read is refused: TestResolveSubmitDeadlineRefusesWhatItCannotUse\n  a deadline must be positive: TestResolveSubmitDeadlineRefusesWhatItCannotUse\n  an expired deadline is not a refusal: TestTimedOutSeparatesAnExpiredDeadlineFromARefusal\n  a refusal is not an expired deadline: TestTimedOutSeparatesAnExpiredDeadlineFromARefusal\n  the flag reaches the invocation: TestSubmitDeadlineComesFromTheFlagThenTheEnvironment, TestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline\n  a refused deadline stops the command: TestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline, TestSubmittingCommandsRefuseAnUnreadableDeadline\n  the submission dials under this invocation's deadline: TestSubmitRequestWaitsForThisInvocationsDeadline\n  the batch weighs the prefix, not one act: TestBatchSubmitRefusalWeighsTheWholePrefix\n  the batch refusal is built from the file: TestBatchReportsTheReplayKeyWhenAnActsDeadlineExpires\n  an act with no key is not called replayable: TestBatchSubmitRefusalWeighsTheWholePrefix\n  publish reads under the deadline it advertises: TestPublicationDecisionReadsUnderThisInvocationsDeadline\n  the adapter reads the environment when it is built: TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment\n  a refused adapter value stops the adapter: TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment\n  the backstop saturates rather than wrapping: TestBackstopOutlastsEvenAnAbsurdDeadline\n  the backstop outlasts the longest deadline: TestBackstopOutlastsEvenAnAbsurdDeadline, TestResidentRequestDeadlinesPreserveCallerCancellation\n  the connector refuses before observing: TestConnectorRefusesAnUnreadableDeadlineBeforeObserving\n  the connector submits under this run's deadline: TestConnectorAppendWaitsForThisRunsDeadline\n\nNothing in this table is green and nothing is excused. Three earlier entries were\nmutants that did not compile, which prove nothing about behaviour; each was\nrewritten as a mutant that builds, and two of the three then exposed real defects,\nrepaired above. Two more survived as compiling mutants because the tests around\nthem could not tell the difference — the batch end-to-end test ran a single act,\nand the connector's dial was never exercised — and both now have fixtures that\ncan.\n\n6. docs/reference/gs/ records the flag on all twelve pages that take it, with the\ndefault and the variable in the same row. gs state carries the fuller account —\nwhen to raise it, and why an expired deadline is not a refusal — and gs batch\nsays that batching does not shorten any act's wait and what its refusal will name.\ndocs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md records the variable for the adapter, which has\nno flags.\n\n7. go vet ./... clean and go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./... green at this exact\nhead, exit 0 read from the process rather than through a pipeline, 37 packages.\n\nOne run before that reported a failure, and it is recorded rather than dropped:\nTestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated in internal/app failed\nonce with \"commit lineage unavailable\" while the machine carried a load average\nof about five. It passes twice in isolation, the package's whole build graph\ncontains nothing this lane touches (go list -deps shows no residentclient), and\nthe second complete run is the green one above. The test builds a thousand\ncommits and reads their lineage through git, so a starved subprocess fits what it\nsaid. I did not call it flaky on one observation, and it is named here so the\nnext person who sees it knows it has been seen.\n\nArchitecture: no layer contract moves. residentclient is the client-side boundary\nfor the local resident and already owned the same shape for actor identity\n(ActorEnvironment, ResolveActor); the deadline joins it as a second setting\nresolved the same way. Nothing changes what the kernel admits, what the fold\ndecides, or what any projection says, and docs/reference/architecture.md is\ntherefore not in this artifact set.","body":{"commit":"7f45a0aa240b3d1c4f9499e01f0a6817ac21de3b","path":"docs/reference/gs/state.md"}}